  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Saturday)\nIssued at 1104 AM MDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nWestern CONUS upper ridge will retrograde and flatten as a strong\nupper trough moves across interior Canada. A strong cold front \nwill cross the region early Monday with cooler temps for mid week.\nHowever, the ridge will rebuild and support hot weather by the \nend of the week, with 90s returning. Lee side trough settling into\nthe FA with winds shifting NW across NW SD and northeast WY. \nBreezy southerly winds will persist ahead of this trough, esp SW \nthrough scentral SD, continuing through tonight as the LLJ \nintensifies. Dry weather expected through most of Monday, with the\nnext chance of rain in the FA expected Monday night into Tuesday \nmorning. Main cold front will move through Monday morning with \nwinds gusting over 30-40 mph through much of the day on the SD \nPlains. A batch of thick low level smoke from OR/WA wildfires is \nexpected to push through the region behind the front tonight into \nMonday per HRRR/RAP forecasts. This may impact visibility for a \nperiod, esp Monday morning. Conds will improve Monday evening as \nthe ll trajectories indicate a more northerly origin to the \nincoming airmass. There is a very small chance that a shower or \nstorm could develop along the cold front in scentral SD late \nMonday afternoon, but left things dry given expected strong \ncapping. Still could see a few showers and TS across the southern \nthird late Monday night as the jet entrance region will overlap \nwith a weak impulse passing to the south of the FA, supporting \nelevated convection. The best chance will be in scentral SD where \nthe better elevated moisture and forcing will be. Temps will fall \ninto the 40s and 50s Monday night with a few 30s expected in \nportions of the higher Black Hills. Highs Tues will be 10 to 15 \ndegrees below seasonal norms mainly in the 70s, with 60s in the \nBlack Hills. Western ridge will gradually rebuild behind the deep \nupper low slowly pushing east across interior Canada. This will \nsupport a warm up with increasing LL moisture as SE return flow \nensues. Expect highs to be back in the 90s by Thur, with triple \ndigits possible by Sat on the SD Plains. Increasing LL moisture \nwill support isolated TS chances by Wed as weak impulses will \nadvect through the region in NW flow. Flow looks to become a \nlittle more active into the following week (after the 10th) as \nsome of the longer range models indicate a stalled frontal \nboundary over the region with faster flow aloft, combined with \nappreciable LL moisture.",
